    "knowledge_point": "Simplifying a Variable Expression Containing a Quotient With Many Factorials",
    "problem": "Given that $k\\ge 1$ is an integer, simplify $\\frac{{k}^{2}(k-1)!}{(k+1)k!}.$",
    "worked_solution": "First, we notice that\n\n$$\nk!=k(k-1)!\n$$\n\nThen, we can cancel the $k(k-1)!$ that occurs in both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{k}^{2}(k-1)!}{(k+1)k!} & =\\frac{k\\cdot k(k-1)!}{(k+1)\\cdot k(k-1)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{k\\cdot \\cancel{k(k-1)!}}{(k+1)\\cdot \\cancel{k(k-1)!}} \\\\  & =\\frac{k}{k+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
